00:16:59.090 --> 00:17:00.230
I got it for one cent on Amazon.

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:01.510
Played it straight for an hour.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:05.370
Doesn't have quite the atmosphere, but it's still pretty cool.

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:08.330
The first time you jump over your first landslide memories.

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:10.710
Memories. I'd give this a solid seven.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:13.510
It's fun and quick. A solid seven out of seven?

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:17.350
Yeah, I mean, yeah, he didn't tell us what the final guideline was.

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:18.490
This is a write in.

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:19.710
It's a write in. OK.

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:21.290
And I'll tell you this.

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:25.370
I was actually surprised at how short this game was.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:36.710
All things considered. And it's also it doesn't have the same padding of length that comes with games like this that are very memory focused.

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:39.710
Like you have to remember the levels to get far without dying.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:47.470
It doesn't have that because you can just continue from your last checkpoint and just brute force your way through this game.

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:53.390
Yeah, I think we should talk about checkpoints for a minute because I think that's one thing that this game does like better than any other game.

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:57.930
I mean, we were like really on board with Ghost and Goblins for having like halfway checkpoints.

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:00.430
Yeah. And I mean, Super Mario Brothers had that, too.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:05.150
But this one kind of has like every, you know, couple meters checkpoints.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:08.950
I'd say that at least I think that every level has like three.

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:11.210
Yeah. Including like the beginning of the level.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:14.310
Right. It's just nice to have those this way.

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:19.970
It's not like if you're at the very end of a level, start from the beginning or even the halfway mark, you get like, you know, two thirds of the way there.

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:25.130
Yeah. But it always just to just to show you what you're doing wrong.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:33.150
It usually has the checkpoint maybe five seconds before like a big jump or a big hazard.

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:37.810
And that's why I didn't mind like the missiles and the rock so much.

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:46.350
I mean, like, yeah, it's a pain in the neck to have gameplay interrupted, but to be able to then just like go back just a little bit and be ready for the missiles and rocks again.

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:49.870
I'm not saying like I aced it the second time every time.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:54.650
Yeah. But I definitely like anticipated it and, you know, made the correct adjustments.

00:18:55.010 --> 00:19:09.770
That's true. I guess like the correct way to play this game is to just restart every time you lose all your lives because you're just going to get a smaller score if you continue from level two or level three.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:14.770
But I mean, because of the way that I grew up playing games, I don't really see that as a big deal.

00:19:14.770 --> 00:19:18.330
But I feel like in the 80s, that was more of a punishment.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:27.390
Yeah. I mean, I guess score resets because then it's like, you know, at the time you would have wanted to submit your score to a magazine or to bring to your friends.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:30.590
And now it's like now I'm just like, look, I'm really just want to beat the game.

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:33.690
Yeah, exactly. And speaking of beating the game, we mentioned that it was short.

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:36.270
I've seen like non speed runs.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:38.430
I don't even know if you could do a speed run of this.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:41.170
I watched I watched the world record speed run of this.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:42.350
Yeah. What was the final time?

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:45.750
I think it was six, six minutes and 47 seconds.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:47.310
And the normal time is 12.

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:52.550
You know, it's just kind of funny that like I mean, and that's a no death situation.

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:58.630
So that kind of makes me wonder, like, well, if Ghost and Goblins was a no death situation, how long would that game be?

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:01.430
And it'd probably be like 25, 30 minutes.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:06.870
But like it's still like double the length of this particular game, which isn't.

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:09.390
And I think it just comes down to the fact that it's not that hard.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:11.650
Yeah, I didn't think it was that hard.

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:14.250
All things considered, mostly because of the continue.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:23.590
But I was I've found a lot of frustration in this game just because I think that these games really aren't my kind of game.

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:30.250
The the memory focused side scroller where it's just remembering patterns of enemies.

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:36.810
And I do like the jump and I do like the the platforming that was introduced into this game to the genre.

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:41.470
But I just find this very frustrating.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:42.970
Yeah. I mean, like I see what you're saying.

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:48.230
And to be fair, it's like people will tell you, well, that's the way those games were designed back then.

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:53.550
But I have to answer that with, well, it's just not a very like it's not a mechanic that ages well.

00:20:53.750 --> 00:20:56.810
No, you know, we don't have that limitation in games today.

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:58.150
And it's for the better.

00:20:58.550 --> 00:21:03.950
Like nobody wants a game where you like I even look at it like a super meat boy or something like that.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:15.390
Right. A game that does have like some memorization aspects to it, but not on like a level where it's it's a random, you know, a random enemy mashup mess kind of thing.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.770
It's like, oh, that that time I hit the landmine.

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:18.710
Oh, this time the missile.

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:24.470
It's like, you know, you kind of know what you did wrong immediately and then go back into the level and do it again.

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:26.450
Exactly. That's not these kind of games.

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:36.290
These memorization focused games hurt them as they get older because no one wants to play that kind of style because it's just intentionally dragging on the game.

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:50.050
Yeah, I think that games like Super Meat Boy and the the more retro focused design, like indie games that have come out in the past decade, they at least allow for some expression in your play.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:58.130
Like you. I mean, yeah, it's like you have to remember certain things about the level, but there are different ways to go about it.

00:21:58.130 --> 00:22:02.610
Whereas in this game, it's just like the scroll happens and the enemy will always be right there.

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:04.470
And I don't know.

00:22:04.550 --> 00:22:05.590
Yeah, I think you're right.

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:07.370
This hasn't it's a it's a feature.

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:10.130
It's a design philosophy that hasn't aged perfectly.

